Z||000000||fiero_nightmare||11-16-2001||10:32 AM||||i have two problems with my 85 GT now. <P>no. 1 is after i bled the clutch i thought i had it fixed but then i found out that if i keep it in the same gear for over 5 minutes the clutch loses all of its pressure. i have to pump up the clutch to get it into gear. the through out bearing makes noise when i let off the clutch pedal. <BR>is that the problem or do i need a new master cylinder too?<P>here is my other problem.<P>as i was testing out my fiero the oil pressure needle dropped to almost ZERO but since i have a minor short in the wires i thought it was the gauge. then after about 20 minutes the engine started to knock. my neibor,my dad and i all think it is the bearings that are now bad. <P>should i just replace the main,rod and cam bearings along with a new oil pump or should i get another engine? <P> the bearings and pump will cost me about $400 total and a used engine is $450. <P>it really makes me mad because i just changed the engine 2 times this year  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> <P>i thought they engine shut off automatically when the oil pressure got too low but for some reason my did not.<P>i must be cursed or something.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero_1.jpg"> <P>85 GT pics <BR>http://communities.msn.com/russcars/85fierogt.msnw||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||11-16-2001||10:59 AM||||It's a very common misconception.  If everything is working as designed the oil pressure switch WILL NOT shut off the pump if the pressure drops.  This is true for all years and engine combinations.  The fuel pump relay will stay engergized by the ECM as long as the engine is running.  The oil pressure switch is a backup for a faulty relay.<BR>Please, Please don't depend on it for a low oil pressure safety cutoff!<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||4
